      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "The notation $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ denotes the greatest integer less than or equal to $x$, or $x$ rounded down to the nearest integer. This is because we want the greatest integer that is still less than or equal to the square root of a number, or the largest integer not exceeding $x$.\n\nThe square root of 1 is 1, so $\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 1 \\rfloor = 1$.\n\nThe square root of 2 is approximately 1.414, so $\\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 1.414 \\rfloor = 1$.\n\nThe square root of 3 is approximately 1.732, so $\\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 1.732 \\rfloor = 1$.\n\nThis continues for all square roots less than or equal to 4 since $\\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 4 \\rfloor = 4$. Continuing with the remaining square roots (5-16), we find that every other one rounds up to 2 due to their uniform distribution in the sequence when taking the squares;\n\nHence, every odd number will be for multiples of 16 because it’s equivalent to $\\sqrt{16(k+1)}$ if we take $k$ as any $2n+1$.\n\nSince we round down for 16 lesser than $n$ even smaller values,\nthe answer to the problem then we're looking for is: $2\\sum_{i=1}^4 k = 2+(2k+2)*3 = \\boxed{14}$. The answer is 14",
